  "application_type_full": "Full planning permission",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development, by reason of sitting, scale and mass would create a visually intrusive, incongruous, overly dominant development, out of keeping with the character and appearance of development along Salisbury Road. The proposal is therefore contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3 and D4 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its siting, bulk, projection and proximity to the site boundary would give rise to an unneighbourly loss of privacy, sunlight and daylight, outlook, as perceived from neighbouring properties including No. 53 Salisbury Road contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3 and D4 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8 and DMD11 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its defensible space, poor quality of outlook, and required measures to ensure privacy for the proposed self-contained flats would result in substandard and inappropriate accommodation incapable of meeting the reasonable needs of occupiers, contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3, D4 and D6 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8 and DMD9 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, in the absence of an adequate Flood Risk Assessment fails to demonstrate how proposed measures manage the risk of flooding, contrary to Section 14 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies SI 13 of the London Plan (2021), CP28 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD59, DMD60 and DMD61 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development would result in additional parking demand within an area already experiencing high levels of parking stress. Insufficient evidence has been provided to demonstrate that the additional demand can be accommodated without resulting in overspill parking and harm to highway safety. The proposal is therefore contrary to Section 9 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policy T6 of the London Plan (2021), DMD 8 and DMD 45 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014)."
  ],
